@name Explainer 10: Planar type 50mm f/2.8
# Explainer #10: Planar type (Paul Rudolph, Zeiss 1896), symmetric: on each
# side of the stop a positive N-SK16 meniscus outside and a thick cemented
# meniscus doublet inside (positive N-SK16 + negative F2), concave towards
# the stop. N-SK16 and F2 have the same index (1.620), so the buried
# cemented surface bends only colour, not the monochromatic rays.
# Role: the buried cemented surfaces correct colour.
# Design: the front half's curvatures, thicknesses and spacings vary,
# mirrored to the rear half (EFL 50 mm, stop gaps >= 3 mm), minimising the
# white-light RMS spot at 0 (double weight), 0.5, 0.7 and 1.0 of the field,
# object at 2.5 m; vignetting towards the corner up to half the light.
# Start: an earlier run of this job from lenses/double-gauss.lens made
# symmetric.
#
# Generated by `lens-render optimize lenses/explainer/10-planar.toml`.
# RMS spot (F, e, C) at f/2.8, object at 2.5 m, by image height
# (fraction of 21.63 mm): 0: 20.5, 0.5: 64.9, 0.7: 72.4, 1: 70.4 µm. Units: mm.
#
# radius      thickness   material       semi-diameter
